{{Politics of Balisca}}

Diplomatic relations between [[Balisca]] and [[Potocsí]] have ranged from two early wars to competition for regional influence. After securing independence from the [[Volisanian Empire]] in the early nineteenth century, Balisca and Potocsí inherited a series of unresolved territorial disputes from their colonial power. The most serious breach in the relationship was the [[Camboriú War]] (1976-1977), sparked by the Potocsino drive to seize disputed territory from Balisca. Diplomatic relations were restored in 1982 with the [[Kuluba Accords]]. 

Despite the numerous periods of both open and muted hostility, the Balisca-Potocsí relationship was not defined by open hostility for most of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. Since the beginning of the 21st century, competition has continued on many levels, and their respective defense policies at times show lingering feelings of mutual suspicion. 

